# The Tale of the Divided River

Long ago, two villages stood on opposite banks of a magical river. The river had a peculiar power: any gift thrown into its waters would triple before reaching the other side.

The villagers of Northbank were cautious. "Why should we send our grain across?" they asked. "The Southbank people might keep it all!" So they sent nothing, and nothing returned.

Southbank villagers felt the same distrust. "If we send first, we'll surely be betrayed!" They too held tight to what they had.

Years of scarcity followed. Both villages watched their resources dwindle.

One day, a child from Northbank didn't know the old fears. She tossed a single apple into the river. It became three on the other side. The Southbank children, delighted, sent back two apples. These became six when they reached Northbank.

Soon both villages understood the river's true lesson: the magic only worked when gifts flowed both ways. The one who gave first sparked abundance, but only reciprocity sustained it.

The villages prospered thereafter, learning that trust rewarded creates more wealth than fear protected. The river's multiplication was not the real magic—it was merely revealing what cooperation had always made possible.
